            <div xml:id="d490">

                <pb facs="0596" n="588" sameAs="frus:frus1941v07/pg588"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.3331/437: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Uruguay</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dawson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Montevideo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-12-24T19:00:00-03:00">December 24, 1941—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 24—6:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>580. Embassy’s telegram No. 567, December 22. Memoranda presented to Embassy this
                    afternoon by Foreign Office confirm oral acceptance of our proposed general
                    provisions contingent upon our <pb facs="0597" n="589" xml:id="pg589"/>
                    willingness, first, to add a paragraph to article II along general lines of that
                    described in paragraph 3 of Embassy’s telegram under reference, second, to
                    suppress paragraph 1 of article XVI and, third, to amend note regarding
                    preferences to contiguous countries to include Bolivia and Paraguay (see
                    paragraph 1, Embassy’s telegram No. 567).</p>
                <p>Uruguayans contend that their basic customs law does not permit any consideration
                    of intent in connection with errors in documentation. (See Embassy’s despatch
                    No. 897 of April 23, 1941.) It is pointed out, however, that the customs
                    regulations permit an importer to examine, weigh, and extract samples from
                    shipments before beginning clearance proceedings. The Department’s early
                    reaction to the Uruguayan proposal with respect to the inclusion of Bolivia and
                    Paraguay in the contiguous country note would be appreciated.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dawson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
